<h1>Unit 26: Recent Trends in Physics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Modern Physics</p><p class="summary">The final unit is about the edge of the subject: the Standard Model and where it stops, the detection of gravitational waves and neutrino oscillations, and the questions that a generation of experiments is now narrowing - slowly, and honestly.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>The Standard Model describes the particles and forces that have been confirmed in the laboratory, but it does not explain dark matter, dark energy, quantum gravity, or the cosmic matter-antimatter imbalance.</li><li>The LHCb collaboration at CERN observed CP violation in baryon decays - announced in March 2025 and published in Nature in July 2025 - adding a piece to the matter-antimatter puzzle, though the Standard Model's CP violation remains far too small to explain the observed imbalance (CERN).</li><li>The Higgs field gives mass to the W and Z bosons and to fermions through the Yukawa coupling, and the Higgs boson was observed at the LHC in 2012.</li><li>Neutrino oscillation proved that neutrinos have mass, which reshaped how the Standard Model is interpreted.</li><li>Gravitational waves were first detected in 2015 by LIGO, opening a new observational window on the universe.</li><li>Experiments in the frontier are statements of probability with controlled systematics: evidence means a statistically significant deviation, not proof.</li><li>The Standard Model accounts for three of the four forces and every confirmed particle, but not dark matter, dark energy, quantum gravity, or the matter-antimatter asymmetry.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Every frontier claim carries an uncertainty; a null result constrains the strength of a coupling rather than proving absence.</li><li>The Standard Model is an effective description validated at the energies tested, and new physics could appear above them without invalidating it.</li><li>Numerical simulation is a third pillar of the subject, and it must be validated against analytically solvable limits and against experiment.</li><li>Observational limits and theoretical predictions live on different timescales; a bound announced by an experiment can tighten long before a theory explains it.</li><li>The Higgs field gives mass to the W and Z bosons and to the fermions, but most of the mass of everyday matter comes from the binding energy inside protons and neutrons, not from the Higgs.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 3 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Particle physics: particles and antiparticles, quarks (baryons and mesons) and leptons (neutrinos)</li><li>Universe: Big Bang and Hubble law — expansion of the Universe</li><li>Dark matter, black hole and gravitational wave</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The Standard Model explains all four fundamental forces.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> It describes the electromagnetic, the strong and the weak interactions with remarkable precision, but gravity is not included, and the neutrino masses and the cosmological constant are not accommodated by the minimal model.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> The Standard Model is a quantum field theory of the electromagnetic, weak and strong interactions; gravity is described separately by general relativity, and no verified quantum theory that includes gravity exists. Its predictions have been tested to extraordinary precision, and no confirmed deviation from it has been found, but it is not a theory of everything.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Antiparticles are exotic heavy partners created only in high-energy laboratories.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The electron's antiparticle, the positron, is the same mass and is found in ordinary cosmic rays and in medical imaging. Every particle of matter has an antiparticle of the same mass.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> For each particle there is an antiparticle of equal mass and opposite charge, lepton number and, for baryons, baryon number. The electron and positron annihilate into photons; quarks and antiquarks do the same, and mesons are themselves quark-antiquark pairs.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Because neutrinos were once thought to be massless, their detection in 2015 settled the question of their mass.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Neutrino oscillation experiments showed that the flavours change along the flight, which requires the mass eigenstates to differ in mass, but the oscillation pattern measures differences of squared masses, not the masses themselves.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Oscillation experiments constrain the differences delta m^2, and the absolute scale is set by other data, principally the shape of the solar neutrino spectrum and the endpoint of electron-neutrino beta decay, as in KATRIN. The current bound is a neutrino mass below about 0.45 eV/c^2, and the ordering of the masses is still an open question.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>Find the de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through a potential difference of 100 V, and compare it with the wavelength of the same electron in its ground state in an atom.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The kinetic energy gained is e V = 100 eV = 1.602 x 10^-17 J, so the momentum is p = sqrt(2 m E) = sqrt(2 x 9.109 x 10^-31 x 1.602 x 10^-17) = sqrt(2.918 x 10^-47) = 5.40 x 10^-24 kg m/s, and the wavelength is lambda = h / p = 6.626 x 10^-34 / 5.40 x 10^-24 = 1.23 x 10^-10 m = 0.123 nm. This is comparable with atomic spacings, which is why the electron shows wave behaviour in the electron microscope. By contrast, the wavelength of an electron bound in an atom with an energy of a few electron volts is of the order of 0.1 to 0.3 nm, and for a nucleus of a few MeV it is of the order of 10^-15 m.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the photoelectric equation from Planck's hypothesis and Einstein's photon model, and state the threshold frequency.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Planck's hypothesis says that a blackbody radiates in quanta of energy E = h nu. Einstein proposed that light itself consists of photons of this energy. In the photoelectric effect an electron absorbs one photon, of energy h nu; part of this goes into the work required to escape from the metal, W = h nu_0, the work function, and the remainder becomes kinetic energy. Conservation of energy therefore gives h nu = W + (1/2) m v_max^2, which is the photoelectric equation. The threshold frequency is the frequency at which the electron just escapes with zero kinetic energy, nu_0 = W / h, so no emission occurs below it however intense the radiation. The equation also explains why the maximum kinetic energy depends on the frequency and not on the intensity, which is the classical prediction the wave theory cannot make.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>Neutrino oscillation was discovered when the measured flux of electron neutrinos from the Sun was about one third of the expected value. Explain how this observation leads to the conclusion that neutrinos have mass, and what the observation does not tell you.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The deficit cannot be explained by loss of flux: the neutral-current measurements show that the total number of neutrinos arriving is essentially the expected one, so the electron neutrinos have changed into other flavours (muon and tau neutrinos), a process called oscillation. Oscillation requires the three neutrino mass eigenstates to have different masses, because the flavour states are superpositions of the mass eigenstates and the phases of the components evolve at different rates, so the probability of finding a given flavour depends on the distance travelled and on the differences of the squared masses. What the observation does not give is the absolute mass: the oscillation probability depends only on delta m^2, so any set of masses with the same differences fits the data equally well, and the absolute scale has to be fixed by other measurements. A further puzzle is the flavour ratio itself, which for the standard solar model should be about 1:2:0, but is measured as about 1:1:1.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Higgs boson mass</td><td>about 125.2 GeV</td></tr><tr><td>Gravitational wave detection (LIGO)</td><td>September 2015</td></tr><tr><td>KATRIN direct bound on the neutrino mass</td><td>0.45 eV/c^2</td></tr><tr><td>Distance to the first gravitational-wave detection, GW150914</td><td>about 1.3 billion light years</td></tr><tr><td>Age of the universe</td><td>about 13.8 billion years</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why is there far more matter than antimatter in the universe?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>CP violation is necessary but, in the Standard Model, far too small; mechanisms such as leptogenesis are candidate explanations under active study (CERN).</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>What drives the accelerating expansion of the universe?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>The observations are secure; the mechanism is not, and no confirmed detection of the responsible particle has been made.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Does gravity admit a consistent quantum description?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>No fully consistent theory exists, and the attempts to build one have not yet produced testable predictions.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Write down the particle content of the Standard Model and identify which observed phenomena it fails to explain.</li><li>Explain how the observation of neutrino oscillation implies a non-zero neutrino mass, and where the mass enters the oscillation formula.</li><li>Take one recent physics result and trace the chain from raw measurement through inference and model to the open question that remains.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/recent-trends-in-physics">Read the chapter notes: Recent Trends in Physics</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-quantum">Quantum 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-modern-suite">Modern physics unit suite 3D</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://home.cern/new-piece-matter-antimatter-puzzle/">CERN: a new piece in the matter-antimatter puzzle</a></li><li><a href="https://www.energy.gov/science/np/articles/katrin-narrows-down-range-neutrinos-mass">US DOE: KATRIN narrows the range of the neutrino mass</a></li><li><a href="https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainssuperconductivity">US DOE: Superconductivity explained</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/dark-matter/">NASA: Dark Matter</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/11-5-the-standard-model">OpenStax: The Standard Model</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/11-6-the-big-bang">OpenStax: The Big Bang</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
